    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar.jpg" alt="Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_portrait_of_Agha_Muhammad_Khan,_Persia,_Qajar,_dated_1795.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sotheby's, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribe settled since Safavid times around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Astarabad</a>, south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, and their chiefs had competed for power in the turmoil that followed the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a>, son of a Qajar chief, had been castrated as a boy on the orders of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid</a> ruler and spent years as a hostage at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>. On Karim Khan’s death in 1779 he escaped, rallied the Qajar tribesmen and began a long struggle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand</a> princes for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> his capital in 1786, and by 1789, the date conventionally used for the start of the dynasty, he was master of northern Iran. In 1794 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and the last Zand ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>, who was blinded and killed. To restore Iranian authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Heraclius II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-krtsanisi">Battle of Krtsanisi</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> in 1795. He was formally crowned shah in 1796 and in the same year took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> from the blind Afsharid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrokh-shah">Shahrokh</a>. In 1797 he was assassinated by his servants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Baba Khan</a> succeeded him as Fath Ali Shah.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lurs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lurs.jpg" alt="Lurs" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lurs">Lurs</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LuristanWomen.17Aug2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shadegan, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> was an agrarian society in which most people were peasants working land owned by the crown, by notables and by religious endowments, usually under sharecropping arrangements. Perhaps a quarter or more of the population were pastoral nomads organised in tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a>, Qashqai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurs">Lurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>, who were also an important source of soldiers. The total population is estimated at some five or six million around 1800 and roughly ten million by the early twentieth century. Towns lived from crafts and trade, organised around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> whose merchants financed commerce and had close ties with the clergy. Raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> was the main export until silkworm disease devastated it in the 1860s, after which carpets, opium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and dried fruit grew in importance, while cheap European textiles damaged local manufacturing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi’a Islam</a> was the state religion, and the Shi’i clergy, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a></em>, gained great independence and influence, since leading jurists, or <em>mujtahids</em>, were regarded as authorities whom believers should follow. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> formed significant minorities, while the Babi movement of the 1840s was crushed and its founder, the Bab, executed in 1850. Iran suffered severe famines in 1870–1872 and again during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when hunger and disease killed a large share of the population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813.jpg" alt="Russo-Persian War (1804–1813)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War (1804–1813)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livebridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franz Roubaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> came to power with a tribal army of cavalry, but they faced the modern armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, tried to create a European-style infantry, the <em>nezam-e jadid</em>, with help from French and British officers, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1804–1813, which ended with the Treaty of Gulistan and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and most of the Caucasian khanates. A second war in 1826–1828, despite early successes, ended in defeat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a>, which surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Erivan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> and fixed the frontier on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Aras</a> river. In the east, Qajar attempts to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, once part of the Safavid realm, failed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-shah-qajar">Mohammad Shah</a> besieged it in vain in 1837–1838, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar">Naser al-Din Shah</a> took it in 1856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war and forced Iran to renounce its claims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1857">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1857. Afterwards the Qajars relied on a small and poorly paid army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade">Persian Cossack Brigade</a>, founded in 1879 under Russian officers, became the most effective unit and was used in 1908 to bombard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">Majlis</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Iran declared neutrality, but Ottoman, Russian and British forces fought on its territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Fath-Ali Shah Qajar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath-Ali Shah Qajar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Fath_Ali_Shah_Standing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mihr 'Ali, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah was in theory an absolute monarch, the ‘Shadow of God on Earth’, whose power was displayed in an elaborate court, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golestan-palace">Golestan Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and portraits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath Ali Shah</a> with his long beard and jewelled crown. In practice central authority was limited. The shah governed through a chief minister, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">sadr</a>-e a’zam</em>, and through royal princes and other governors sent to the provinces, who collected taxes, often through tax farmers, and kept much of the revenue. Offices were frequently sold, tribal chiefs ruled their own people and local notables and clergy wielded strong influence in towns. Reformers tried to strengthen the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-kabir">Amir Kabir</a>, chief minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar">Naser al-Din Shah</a> in 1848–1851, reorganised finances, curbed abuses and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-ul-funun">Dar ol-Fonun</a>, a polytechnic college in Tehran, before he was dismissed and put to death in 1852. Later ministers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-hosein-khan-sepahsalar">Mirza Hosein Khan Moshir od-Dowleh</a> pursued reforms in the 1870s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Constitutional Revolution</a> transformed the system: in 1906 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a> granted a constitution and an elected parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">Majlis</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supplementary-fundamental-law">Supplementary Fundamental Law</a> of 1907 established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with guarantees of rights, although the clergy were given a role in reviewing legislation for its conformity with Islamic law.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tekyeh-dowlat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tekyeh-dowlat.jpg" alt="Tekyeh Dowlat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tekyeh-dowlat">Tekyeh Dowlat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Courtyard_in_Golestan_Palace.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HanSilo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> period shaped modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in lasting ways. The dynasty fixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> as the capital, and the frontiers it lost or accepted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the Gulf largely define Iran’s borders today. The treaties of Gulistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Turkmenchay</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> under Russian rule, separating them from the Iranian world. Qajar Iran became an arena of rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>, and concessions to foreign companies, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reuter-concession">Reuter concession</a> of 1872 and the oil concession granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-knox-darcy">William Knox D’Arcy</a> in 1901, provoked lasting resentment of foreign influence; the D’Arcy concession led to the discovery of oil at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjed-soleyman">Masjed Soleyman</a> in 1908 and the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">Anglo-Persian Oil Company</a>. The Tobacco Protest of 1891–1892 and the Constitutional Revolution, among the first such movements in Asia, showed the power of alliances between merchants, clergy and intellectuals that reappeared in later Iranian politics. The period also saw the first Persian newspapers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-ul-funun">Dar ol-Fonun</a> and new schools, and a distinctive artistic culture, including large oil portraits, lacquer painting and photography, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar">Naser al-Din Shah</a> practised himself. The <em>ta’zieh</em> passion plays commemorating the martyrdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Imam Hosein</a> flourished, and in 1868 the shah built a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> for them, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekyeh-dowlat">Takyeh Dowlat</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitutional order was fragile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>, who came to the throne in 1907, opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">Majlis</a>, and in June 1908 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade">Persian Cossack Brigade</a> shelled the parliament building. Constitutionalists held out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a>, and in July 1909 forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> tribes took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and deposed the shah in favour of his young son Ahmad Shah. Foreign pressure continued: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> of 1907 divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> into Russian and British spheres of influence, and in 1911 a Russian ultimatum forced the dismissal of the American financial adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morgan-shuster">Morgan Shuster</a> and the closure of the Majlis. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> neutral Iran was occupied by rival armies and devastated by famine. After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-agreement">Anglo-Persian Agreement</a> of 1919, which the Majlis never ratified, while separatist and revolutionary movements arose in Gilan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. On 21 February 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>, an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>, seized Tehran in a coup with the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zia-ol-din-tabatabaee">Sayyed Zia al-Din Tabataba’i</a>. As minister of war and from 1923 prime minister, he built a national army and restored central control. On 31 October 1925 the Majlis deposed Ahmad Shah, who was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and in December a constituent assembly made Reza Khan shah, founding the Pahlavi dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> fixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> as Iran’s capital and much of its modern frontiers while losing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, became a battleground of British and Russian influence, and produced the Tobacco Protest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Constitutional Revolution</a> of 1906, which gave Iran its first parliament and constitution.</em></p>

